% Inline, params and debug are three ways of joining one part list, not three
% code paths. The renderer never concatenates a literal into a string.

### name: mode.inline.is-the-default
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T > 100 AND T < 200
--- expect
((`t` > 100) AND (`t` < 200))
===
### name: mode.params.placeholders-in-order
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T & "a" & "b"
--- expect
CONCAT(CONCAT(`t`, ?), ?)
--- params
t"a", t"b"
===
### name: mode.params.a-number-is-never-a-placeholder
--- note
No coercion of a bound string reproduces a bare decimal literal. CAST(? AS
DECIMAL(65,10)) pads the scale, so TRIM(2.50) answered "2.5000000000"; (? + 0)
drops the scale and floats above seventeen digits; and with neither, (? = ?)
compares two strings and 2.50 = 2.5 is FALSE. MariaDB reads the literal 2.50 as
DECIMAL with scale 2, and only the literal does.

Safe because of Emit::numericLiteral: the characters a NUM literal can contain
are digits, one dot and a leading minus, by construction. It is the one value
form that provably cannot carry a quote or a comment, so it is the one params
mode has nothing to protect.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T > 100 AND T < 200
--- expect
((`t` > 100) AND (`t` < 200))
--- params

===
### name: mode.params.a-num-value-binding-inlines-too
--- note
Not only literals from the AST: a host value declaring type NUM takes the same
path, and binds nothing.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}, "LIMIT": {"kind": "value", "value": "2.50", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T > LIMIT
--- expect
(`t` > 2.50)
--- params

===
### name: mode.params.text-and-numbers-together
--- note
The numbers inline, the text binds, and the placeholder ordinals count only the
placeholders actually emitted.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
LEFT(T, 3) $== "abc"
--- expect
(CAST(LEFT(`t`, 3) AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
--- params
t"abc"
===
### name: mode.debug.slots-are-visible
--- note
debug numbers what params would bind, so a NUM literal is absent from both.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
debug
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
T & "a" & "b"
--- expect
CONCAT(CONCAT(`t`, ~1~), ~2~)
===
### name: mode.params.a-literal-holding-a-placeholder-stays-a-literal
--- note
The reason a part list exists. Inline, the ? is inside a quoted string; in
params mode the real placeholder is a separate slot and the two never mix.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
S $== "?"
--- expect
(CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
--- params
t"?"
===
### name: mode.params.same-expression-inline
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "column", "column": "s", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
S $== "?"
--- expect
(CAST(`s` AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('?' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: mode.params.order-follows-the-text-not-the-tree
--- note
The condition is a BOOL literal and inlines; only the two branches bind. Their
order in `params` follows the TEXT, which is what Fragment::bindings() walking
the output is for.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- source
COND(TRUE, "a", "b")
--- expect
CASE WHEN TRUE THEN ? ELSE ? END
--- params
t"a", t"b"
===
### name: mode.params.a-bool-is-never-a-placeholder
--- note
A BOOL renders as Map::lexical('true'|'false') -- a token out of the dialect
document, not out of a rule -- so there is nothing for a placeholder to protect.
Binding it as a string breaks SQLite outright: 1 = '1' is 0 there, because
INTEGER and TEXT are different storage classes and no affinity applies to a bare
parameter. TRUE XOR TRUE answered TRUE in params mode and FALSE inline until
this. Found by the fuzz lane on sqlite's first run.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- mode
params
--- source
TRUE XOR TRUE
--- expect
((1) <> (1))
--- params

===
### name: mode.params.literal-needle-in-a-list
--- note
Every slot must appear exactly once. The needle is rendered per comparison, so
a three-element IN with a literal needle binds six values for six placeholders
— not one value spliced three times, which is what a driver rejects.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- source
"a" IN ("a", "b", "c")
--- expect
(((CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin) OR (CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)) OR (CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST(? AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin))
--- params
t"a", t"a", t"a", t"b", t"a", t"c"
===
### name: mode.params.join-separator-is-bound-per-gap
--- dialect
mariadb
--- mode
params
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a"}, {"column": "b"}, {"column": "c"}]}}
--- source
JOIN(V, "-")
--- expect
CONCAT(CONCAT(CONCAT(CONCAT(`a`, ?), `b`), ?), `c`)
--- params
t"-", t"-"
